10:46:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Newspapers are cheap because they are made with a completely different printing process and business model than your home printer. - **They don’t use inkjet cartridges.** Home printers spray tiny droplets from expensive, highly engineered cartridges. Cartridge ink is notoriously overpriced—often more expensive per litre than luxury liquids—because manufacturers often sell printers cheaply and make profit on supplies. - **Newspapers use high-speed offset presses.** A newspaper press makes metal printing plates for each page and then runs huge rolls of paper through rollers at enormous speed. The ink is a relatively cheap, thick paste transferred mechanically, not precision-sprayed. - **Scale changes everything.** Setting up a press is expensive, but once it is running, printing an additional copy costs very little. A press may produce tens of thousands of copies per hour. The cost of plates and setup is spread across the whole run. - **They buy materials in bulk.** Publishers buy ink by the drum or tanker load and paper by the truckload. They also use cheap, thin **newsprint**, designed for mass printing rather than quality or durability. - **Newspaper colour is usually CMYK process colour.** Four ink colours—cyan, magenta, yellow, and black—are layered to create most colours. It is fast and cheap at scale, though usually less vivid or sharp than glossy magazine printing. - **The cover price is not the full funding source.** Traditionally, advertising paid for much of a newspaper’s production and distribution. The price you pay at the shop may be below the actual cost of printing, delivery, reporting, and retail handling. Today many papers also rely on subscriptions, digital revenue, sponsorships, or owners willing to sustain them. So if a paper sells for, say, $1–$3, that does not mean it cost only that much to make—and your home printer’s ink cost is not comparable to industrial press ink. Printing one newspaper-sized colour document at home is about the least economical possible way to do it.
