10:46:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right at the basic physical level: both put coloured pigment onto paper. The huge price difference is not because newspaper ink is magical; it is mostly because your printer is an extraordinarily inefficient and expensive *retail delivery system for ink*. Think of it less like bicycle vs motorcycle and more like: - buying water in tiny branded single-use bottles from a convenience shop, versus - piping water from a reservoir into an industrial bottling line. It is still water. But the cost per litre is wildly different. A home inkjet cartridge includes far more than ink: - a precision print head/nozzles in many models; - electronics, chips, and calibration; - tiny sealed chambers and packaging; - distribution, retailer margins, and inventory costs; - replacement for ink that gets used in cleaning cycles or dries in the machine; - and, importantly, printer manufacturers’ profit model: inexpensive printer, expensive consumables. The actual dye/pigment may be a small fraction of the cartridge’s retail price. A newspaper printer buys fairly simple offset ink in barrels, uses it in a continuous machine, and wastes very little per printed copy once the run is underway. It does not need hundreds or thousands of separately packaged cartridges, each capable of being installed by a consumer and producing photographic-quality dots. There is also a major technical distinction in *how* the ink gets down: - Your inkjet printer must accurately fire microscopic droplets through nozzles, often at high resolution—say 1,200 dpi or more—and may use lots of ink to make dense colours or photos. - A newspaper offset press transfers a thin film of cheap ink from rollers to a plate, then to paper. It is optimised to repeat the same pages hundreds of thousands of times, often at lower visual quality and on absorbent paper. And newspapers generally use surprisingly little ink by mass. A full colour newspaper may look ink-heavy, but each layer is thin. The expensive part of a home print is not necessarily the quantity of pigment on the page; it is the cartridge system and its pricing. So: yes, both are fundamentally “ink on paper.” But buying 2–10 mL of specially packaged inkjet ink at consumer prices can cost more than a newspaper company buying the same usable amount of bulk press ink for hundreds or thousands of copies.
