10:46:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: At the highest level, yes: both create an image by depositing coloured material in a pattern on paper. But “ink getting pressed onto paper” is specifically much closer to a newspaper press than to an inkjet printer. - **Newspaper offset press:** ink is physically carried on rollers, transferred from a printing plate to a rubber blanket, then pressed onto the moving paper. It is a mechanical contact process. The same image is repeated from the same plate tens or hundreds of thousands of times. - **Home inkjet:** there is normally no pressing. The printer ejects microscopic liquid droplets through tiny nozzles, usually using heat pulses or piezoelectric actuators, onto paper without contact. It must individually decide and fire millions or billions of droplets for every sheet. - **Laser printer:** different again: electrically charged toner powder is attracted to an image on a drum, transferred to paper, then melted/fused onto it. So there is a genuinely different production method, not merely a faster version of your home printer. That said, you are also right that **scale and pricing are most of the explanation for the startling cost difference**. Industrial printing is not violating any economic or physical law. A newspaper company can buy ordinary press ink cheaply in bulk and spread setup costs over a massive run. Your cartridge is a tiny, packaged, precision-engineered consumable sold through a consumer retail chain, often under a business model that subsidised the original printer. If you somehow took the *same mass of ordinary black or CMYK ink* used in a newspaper page and bought it in a large container, it would not cost anything remotely like 20 newspaper cover prices. The expensive thing is mostly not “the coloured substance”; it is buying it as home-printer ink in a cartridge and using it in a low-volume, high-precision device.
