10:47:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair enough. At the abstraction level you mean, they are the same method: > Put controlled amounts of coloured material onto paper in a pattern. A newspaper press, an inkjet, a laser printer, a stamp, and even screen printing all do that. The differences in *how* they control and apply the colour matter to engineers, but they do not change the basic idea. And your main intuition is correct: the reason a newspaper can be cheap is not that newspaper companies have found some fundamentally different way to make ink-and-paper images. It is mainly economics: 1. **Inkjet cartridges are sold at extreme markup.** The actual ink in a cartridge is cheap relative to its retail cost. Printer makers commonly make much of their profit after the printer sale. 2. **Home printers waste ink.** They purge nozzles, run cleaning cycles, leave ink in cartridges, and may reject cartridges as “empty” before every last usable drop is accessible. 3. **Bulk ink is cheap.** If you bought compatible refill ink in bottles—or bulk commercial printing ink—the per-page ink cost would drop dramatically. It would still not be newspaper-press cheap, but it would be nowhere near official-cartridge pricing. 4. **A newspaper page is made extremely cheaply at volume.** The press setup is shared across perhaps tens or hundreds of thousands of copies. Paper comes in giant rolls, ink in drums, and the machine runs continuously. 5. **The newspaper’s sale price is subsidised.** Advertising, subscriptions, and other revenue pay part of the bill. The cover price is not simply “paper + ink + printing cost.” So yes: “it’s just ink on paper” is a valid summary. The apparent paradox comes from comparing the retail cost of a few millilitres of proprietary consumer inkjet ink with the industrial cost of bulk commodity printing ink, plus a product whose sticker price may be partly paid by advertisers.
